The page holds three unrelated blocks: (A) top third, genus-2 period and theta notes keyed to page references; (B) middle, plane cubics with a double point; (C) bottom, the cube of a binary quartic.

(A) Genus-2 hyperelliptic periods (top third).
- The header reads in the image like '∞ > κ² > λ² > μ²'. The diagram lists the real branch points in the order -∞(=∞), 0, 1, 1/κ², 1/λ², 1/μ². That order requires 1 > κ² > λ² > μ² > 0, so the first symbol is probably '1' (or a sloppy '∞').
- This is Rosenhain's normal form of a genus-2 curve: y² = x(1-x)(1-κ²x)(1-λ²x)(1-μ²x).
- The marginal references 'p. 409', 'p. 417', 'p. 434' fall inside the page range of Rosenhain's 'Mémoire sur les fonctions de deux variables et à quatre périodes' (Mém. Sav. Étrangers XI, 1851, pp. 361-468). I take them to be references to that memoir. This identification is my conjecture, not certain.
- The theta written 'ϑ(r over r,s)(v,w)' at p. 409 matches Rosenhain-style thetas with characteristics in two variables v, w.
- Two schematic real-axis diagrams record the values of two normalized integrals u_1, u_2 on the segments between consecutive branch points. The visible entries are 0, cπ/2 (also c·π/2), ½ log q for u_2 and ½ log p for u_1. These are half-periods expressed through the nomes p, q of the theta series, so the periods are of the type log p, log q, cπi. 'A' marks a base point or region.
- The second diagram draws loops around the pairs (1/κ², 1/λ²) and (1/λ², 1/μ²). They are labelled 2a, 2b and a_{1,1}, a_{1,2}, i.e. the period moduli of the normalized integral u_1 along those cycles.
- A small rectangle with corners 0 and ∞ and sides marked 1 sketches the cut, simply connected surface (a fundamental polygon).
- Next comes the increment of log ϑ − log ϑ' across the cuts. Across the first cut it is 2(Σ(α_1^1+α_1^2) − Σ(β_1^1+β_1^2)) − a_{1,1}; across the second it is 2Σ(α_2^1+α_2^2) − Σ(β_2^1+β_2^2) − a_{1,2}; for the remaining cuts it is 0, 0.
- Here α, β are the coordinates of the integral sums at the zeros of the two thetas. This is the standard Riemann (1857) bookkeeping: the jump of a quotient of thetas equals the Abel sums of the zeros minus a period. Riemann's factor-2 conventions are kept as written.
- The line log(ϑ) − log ϑ' + u_1 adds the normalized integral to make a single-valued combination.
- A German note reads roughly 'Wechsel ∞ 0 1 1/κ² 1/λ² 1/μ² in der Mitte ... verschiedenen ...'. It is a remark about permuting the branch points; the reading is uncertain.

